title: Fix resource exhaustion when replaying finalized-block transactions
doc:
- audience:
  - Node Dev
  - Runtime Dev
  description: |-
    # Description

    Re-applying the transactions of a finalized block can yield different results than the
    original execution: transactions that originally succeeded are rejected with
    `ExhaustsResources`. This affects runtime APIs that replay a block (`pallet-revive`'s
    `trace_block` / `trace_tx`), where the rejected tail transactions then produce no trace
    (a missing or zero-value phantom entry, depending on the tracer).

    **The cause.** Each extrinsic is charged its declared (worst-case) `proof_size`;
    `StorageWeightReclaim` then refunds the difference between that and the actual proof size,
    which it reads from a proof-size recorder. The original execution (at authoring) has a
    recorder registered, so the over-charge is reclaimed and the block fits. The replay
    registers no recorder, so the proof-size lookup returns nothing and reclaim is skipped. The
    declared `proof_size` accumulates past the block limit and `CheckWeight` rejects the tail
    extrinsics. Reproduced in an Asset Hub Westend integration test.

    **The fix.** Replay the block through the runtime API with a proof-size recorder
    registered, so replay reproduces the original execution and the whole block is processed.

    # Integration

    - New **unsafe-gated** RPC `state_callRecorded` on `StateApi`: the recorded sibling of
      `state_call`, with the same `(method, call_data, at)` shape. The caller provides the
      complete SCALE-encoded arguments; the node executes them with a proof-size recorder
      registered and does not inspect or amend them, so the method is agnostic to runtime API
      payload shapes (including versioned payloads).
    - New `TracingExecuteBlock::call_recorded(at, method, call_data)` trait method with a
      default impl that errors (non-breaking); implemented for parachains in
      `cumulus-client-service`. This node-internal hook is the durable interface: an in-node
      eth-rpc (polkadot-sdk#11297) can consume it directly, making the RPC a transitional
      bridge.
    - New `Error::CallRecordedUnsupported` and wire code `CALL_RECORDED_UNSUPPORTED_ERROR_CODE`
      in `sc-rpc-api`.
    - `sc-rpc-api`'s state `Error::UnsafeRpcCalled` now reports the standard "method not found"
      code (-32601), per the `DenyUnsafe` design (a denied unsafe method is indistinguishable
      from an absent one). Clients use this to fall back.
    - The trace types in `pallet-revive-types` now deserialize the JSON they serialize: fields
      skipped when empty (`skip_serializing_if`) also carry `#[serde(default)]`. Previously a
      serialized trace with any empty field could not be read back by typed clients.

    # Review Notes

    **Approach.** eth-rpc fetches the block (it already needs it), encodes the runtime API
    arguments itself, and calls `state_callRecorded` at the parent hash. A fresh proof-size
    recorder is always used: replay is a pure function of the block and parent state, identical
    on every node.

    **Why a new RPC, not the existing call path.** `pallet-revive-eth-rpc` talks to the node
    remotely, so its typed `runtime_api.trace_block()` already crosses the wire as a
    `state_call`; the recorder can only be registered node-side. Registering a recorder on
    every `state_call` would put recording overhead on all hot paths (e.g. metadata fetches)
    and change observable behaviour for all callers; the scoped method gives per-call consent.

    **Client fallback.** When a node cannot service `state_callRecorded` (method absent,
    unsafe RPC methods disabled, or no recorder hook — e.g. a solochain), eth-rpc falls back to
    the plain typed `trace_block`/`trace_tx`. The fallback is not silent: version skew and
    unsafe-disabled warn (the recorder-less replay can still drop the tail on PoV chains), the
    expected recorder-less case logs at debug. Degradation is also visible to consumers: on the
    replay that can drop traces, `debug_traceBlock*` returns dropped transactions as geth-style
    `{txHash, error}` entries instead of silently omitting them, and `debug_traceTransaction`
    returns a trace-unavailable error instead of "not found".

    **Tests.** Integration tests in `asset-hub-westend` reproduce the bug and confirm the fix
    through the versioned trace APIs. A block of two storage-heavy revive calls is replayed,
    and the tail is traced only with a recorder registered. A new `repeated_storage_read`
    fixture creates the `proof_size` over-charge that triggers the drop: it reads a distinct
    (cold) storage key per round, since per-transaction cold/hot access pricing bills only cold
    accesses at worst-case proof size. Unit tests in `pallet-revive-eth-rpc` cover the fallback
    (which errors downgrade to a recorder-less replay vs. propagate); `sc-rpc` tests pin the
    unsafe-denied wire contract and the dispatch-failure error mapping.
crates:
- name: sc-tracing
  bump: minor
- name: cumulus-client-service
  bump: minor
- name: sc-rpc-api
  bump: major
- name: sc-rpc
  bump: major
- name: pallet-revive-eth-rpc
  bump: patch
- name: pallet-revive
  bump: patch
- name: asset-hub-westend-runtime
  bump: patch
- name: pallet-revive-fixtures
  bump: patch
- name: pallet-revive-types
  bump: patch
